Contributing to Badcoin

Badcoin is an open, community-driven modernization of an older multi-algorithm blockchain. Anyone may contribute: peer review, testing, refactoring, documentation, or code patches. What matters is clarity, quality, and respect for the long-term stability of the chain.

This document outlines how contributions are structured, evaluated, and merged.

Project Structure & Governance

Badcoin follows an open contributor model. There are no “Core developers” with special privileges, but for practical reasons:

Maintainers merge pull requests and enforce project standards.

The lead maintainer oversees releases, consensus-level decisions, and appoints maintainers.

Trust is earned over time through meaningful, reliable contributions.
No single developer “owns” the project.

Contributor Workflow

All contributions, without exception, follow the pull-request model.

1. Fork the repository
2. Create a topic branch

Use descriptive patterns:

feature/<name> for new functionality

bugfix/<name> for fixes

refactor/<name> for code cleanup

docs/<name> for documentation

testnet/<name> for testnet activity

release/<version> for version prep

Example:
feature/wallet-integration

3. Commit your changes

Keep commits atomic

Do not mix formatting changes with functional changes

Follow the coding conventions documented in doc/developer-notes.md

4. Write proper commit messages

Short subject (≤ 50 chars)

Blank line

One or more paragraphs explaining why the change exists

Reference issues using refs #1234 or fixes #4321

5. Push and open a Pull Request

Prefix PR titles with the affected area:

Consensus: <description>

Net: / P2P:

RPC: / REST: / ZMQ:

Mining:

Wallet:

Qt:

Scripts: / Tools:

Tests:

Docs:

Trivial: for PRs that do not alter executable code

Example:
Wallet: Fix null keypool import crash

Use [WIP] if the PR is not ready for merging.

Pull Request Requirements

All pull requests must:

Compile cleanly

Pass unit tests and functional tests

Follow project coding style

Contain only focused, related changes

Include documentation updates when needed

Consensus-affecting changes require extensive discussion, review, and justification.

PRs that refactor or move code must not change behavior without explicit purpose.

Squashing & Rebasing

You may be asked to squash commits before merging.

git checkout your_branch
git rebase -i HEAD~n   # n = number of commits
git push -f


If rebasing terrifies you, enable “Allow edits from maintainers” and someone can help.

Peer Review Philosophy

Peer review is essential and expected. Reviewers will test patches, examine code, and comment with:

ACK – tested and approved

utACK – reviewed, not tested

Concept ACK – idea makes sense

NACK – technical reasons the change should not be merged

Nit – minor, non-blocking suggestions

Review takes time. Be patient. Quality matters more than speed.

Coding Style

Badcoin inherits the Bitcoin Core/Myriaid conventions:

C++11

No unnecessary dependencies

Minimal stylistic divergence

Clean diffs

Avoid format-only PRs combined with logic changes

Trivial changes that create noise without benefit may be closed.

Release Policy

The project lead is responsible for:

Release planning

Tagging

Final merge approval for release branches

Licensing

By contributing to this repository, you agree that:

Your work is licensed under the MIT License, unless explicitly stated otherwise

If you contribute code you did not author, you must preserve its original copyright/license header

See COPYING for the full license text

Thank You

Every sensible contribution helps modernize a strange, aging, five-algorithm blockchain held together by hope, caffeine, and sheer stubbornness. Your clean patches keep Badcoin from collapsing into a flaming ball of legacy spaghetti.